  "sources": {
   "mr_primary": "libnumbertext mr.sor (hard-coded 0-100): https://sources.debian.org/src/libnumbertext/1.0.11-4/data/mr.sor",
   "mr_variants": "https://www.shalusharma.com/2024/09/marathi-numbers-1-to-100.html ; https://marathi.indiatyping.com/index.php/marathi-typing-tips-free/marathi-counting (0-20)",
   "hi_primary": "https://www.vedantu.com/general-knowledge/how-to-count-1-to-100-in-hindi ; https://www.schooldekho.org/school/blog/details/1-to-100-in-hindi-1496",
   "hi_romanised_check": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hindustani_numerals (IAST table 0-99, after McGregor 1987)"
  },
  "matching_advice": [
   "First form in each list is the standard Devanagari spelling; later Devanagari entries are attested/common variants; Latin entries are lowercase romanised variants.",
   "Normalise input and lexicon the same way: NFC, lowercase Latin, strip ZWJ/ZWNJ (U+200D/U+200C) and nukta (U+093C), fold chandrabindu (U+0901) to anusvara (U+0902).",
   "Marathi eyelash-ra: 74/84/94 are listed as ऱ्य (U+0931), र्‍य (with ZWJ) and र्य; after ZWJ stripping the last two coincide.",
   "Digits: map Devanagari ०-९ (U+0966-U+096F) to ASCII before numeric parsing; Chrome often returns digits instead of words.",
   "Multi-word forms exist ('एक सौ', 'ek sau', 'हां जी', 'theek hai'): use longest match over tokens.",
   "Fraction prefixes are often glued to the number in Marathi (साडेतीन, सव्वादोन, पावणेचार, साडेनऊ): strip a known fraction prefix from a token, then look up the remainder.",
   "Marathi 'एकशे' (one hundred as compound prefix, e.g. एकशे वीस = 120) and 'शे' suffix (दोनशे = 200) are not in the 0-100 table; heights max 125 so handle 'एकशे X' / 'ek so X' / 'sau X' / 'एक सौ X' as 100+X."
  ],
  "ambiguities": [
   "100: Hindi सौ/sau vs Marathi शंभर/shambhar. Romanised 'sau' is near Marathi 'sava/savva' (+0.25); match whole tokens only.",
   "Many numbers share spelling and value across hi and mr (एक, तीन, चार, सात, आठ, तीस, साठ, सत्तर, sattar, chalis...). Harmless.",
   "9: Hindi नौ, Marathi नऊ; 5: Hindi पांच, Marathi पाच (Marathi lists also show पांच).",
   "Marathi 61-68: both ष्ट and ष्ठ spellings in use (एकसष्ट / एकसष्ठ); libnumbertext uses ष्ट.",
   "Marathi 19: एकोणीस (standard) vs एकोणवीस (one list).",
   "Marathi 99: one source gives 'एकोणशंभर'; standard is नव्याण्णव. Not included.",
   "Hindi 23 'teis' vs 30 'tees' and Hindi 33 'taintis' vs 35 'paintis' / 37 'saintis' are near-homophones; expect ASR confusion.",
   "Romanised 'no' is deliberately not listed for Hindi 9 (clashes with confirm_no); 'nav' for Marathi 9 also means 'name'/'new' in some contexts.",
   "Marathi 'हा'/'ha' means 'this' as well as a weak 'yes'.",
   "'paav'/'पाव' alone = 0.25 ('paav kilo' = 250 g), but 'पावणे'/'paune' = minus 0.25 of the following number.",
   "Hindi normally uses डेढ़/ढाई for 1.5/2.5, not 'saadhe ek'/'saadhe do'; 'saadhe' appears with 3+. Marathi likewise uses दीड/अडीच.",
   "'saade' (साडे, Marathi) and 'saadhe' (साढ़े, Hindi) both mean +0.5.",
   "Romanised Marathi 'tis'/'tees' (30) also equal Hindi 30 - same value, fine; romanised 'sat' = 7 in both."
  ]
 },
